  frames:
    count: exactly 4 hollow square frames, non-overlapping
    size: all same outer size S in 6-8 (inner side = S-2 in 4-6)
    colors: one border color shared by all 4 frames, OR two colors split 2+2 (one color per diagonal pair) [observed]
    layout: roughly one frame per quadrant of the grid; frame centers not aligned to a strict row/col grid [observed]
    interiors: |
      - 1 frame has a fully-blank interior (all background, reads as 0 inside).
      - 2 frames form the training pair (s1.s, s1.d): their non-zero interior color-sets are identical.
      - 1 frame is the test sprite (s2): its non-zero interior colors are DIFFERENT from the training pair's.
  training_pair_rule:
    kind: one of {endpoints, fill, sym_fill, comp_checker, invert0, sym (rotation/reflection)}; pair must be related by exactly one such transform
    palette: pair interiors use 1-2 non-background colors drawn from {1,2,3,4,5,6,8} (not equal to frame color or bg colors)
    pixel_count: each pair interior has 3-32 non-zero pixels; s1.s and s1.d pixel counts may differ (e.g. endpoints/fill)
  test_sprite (s2):
    palette: 1-2 colors, distinct from the training pair's color set but same count pattern
    pixel_count: 3-32 non-zero pixels, chosen so the same rule yields a valid (not trivial) transformed output
  output: inner grid of the test frame (S-2) x (S-2) after applying the inferred rule to s2 (this is the target only; the generator emits only the input).
  background_noise: |
    bg is not a perfectly clean stripe; ~30-50% of bg cells are perturbed with random palette colors or extra noise digits (including 0 [observed] and non-frame colors), provided no large hollow square of a single color accidentally appears outside the 4 intended frames.

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first — build the 4 sprite interiors (s1.s, s1.d, s2, blank) then place inside frames, then render background.
  steps:
    - Pick grid H=W=30, frame count=4, outer size S in 6-8 (inner side n = S-2).
    - Pick a transformation rule R from {endpoints, fill, sym_fill, comp_checker, invert0, sym}. Build s1.s as a random n x n sprite with 1-2 colors {v, optional stable c} from palette P1 subset {1,2,3,4,5,6,8}; derive s1.d = R(s1.s). Ensure s1.d differs from s1.s and has >=1 non-zero pixel.
    - Build s2 as a random n x n sprite using 1-2 colors P2 disjoint from P1 and from frame color; choose P2 so R(s2) is non-degenerate (not all zero, not equal to s2). Build the blank interior = all zeros.
    - Pick frame border color(s): either one color for all 4 frames, or two colors (one per diagonal pair). Frame colors disjoint from P1, P2, and bg palette.
    - Pick 4 non-overlapping axis-aligned square regions of side S, one per quadrant, with small jitter. Assign s1.s, s1.d, s2, blank randomly to the four positions.
    - Render background first: pick a period-4 or period-5 palette of 4-5 colors disjoint from frame colors and sprite colors; paint g[r,c] = palette[(r +/- c) mod p]. Then perturb ~30-50% of bg cells with random palette / extra-noise values (avoid creating any hollow single-color square of side >=4 outside the intended frames).
    - Draw the 4 frame borders (overwriting bg). Paint interior background to 0 (black), then stamp the sprite pixels into each interior.
    - Verify: solver finds exactly 4 frames, identifies the pair by matching color sets, infers rule R, and produces a non-trivial output. Reject and retry if any check fails.
